::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which focus is strongest for an informative speech plan?
{
=A. Organized preparation helps students deliver clearer presentations.#Correct. This is specific and can guide related main points.
~B. Presentations and other things.#This is vague and not focused.
~C. I might talk about school.#This does not give a clear informative purpose.
~D. Many topics are interesting.#This is broad and does not guide the speech.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which set best develops one informative point?
{
~A. Point only, with no support.#This is incomplete because the audience needs explanation.
=B. Point, support, reasoning.#Correct. This gives the idea, a detail, and an explanation of why it matters.
~C. Joke, topic change, ending.#This does not develop information.
~D. Unrelated fact, unrelated fact, unrelated fact.#Unrelated facts do not support a point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which action shows accurate paraphrasing for speech notes?
{
~A. Copy the source exactly into the notes.#Copying does not show paraphrasing.
~B. Change the meaning to make it sound stronger.#Changing meaning makes the paraphrase inaccurate.
~C. Remove the main idea from the source.#Removing the main idea makes the note inaccurate because the speaker loses the source's central idea.
=D. Restate the source idea in new wording while keeping the meaning.#Correct. Accurate paraphrase keeps meaning and changes wording.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which item belongs in a speech-ready format checklist?
{
~A. Main points are unrelated.#Unrelated points weaken format and focus.
~B. Notes are impossible to follow.#A quality format should be easy to use.
~C. The speaker copies every source sentence.#Copying does not create speech-ready notes.
=D. The plan includes focus, points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. These parts support quality speech format.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which main point supports the focus "Preparation improves presentation clarity"?
{
=A. Outlining ideas helps the audience follow the order.#Correct. This supports how preparation improves clarity.
~B. Lunch schedules can be confusing.#This is unrelated to presentation clarity.
~C. Some rooms have chairs.#This does not support the focus.
~D. Music can be relaxing.#This does not connect to the speech focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support is most specific?
{
~A. Practice is good.#This is too general because it does not give a concrete detail that supports the speech point.
~B. Notes help.#This is vague and underdeveloped.
=C. A timer can show whether the introduction takes longer than planned.#Correct. This gives a specific support detail.
~D. Speeches exist.#This does not support a point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition is most purposeful?
{
~A. Next thing.#This is vague and does not show connection.
~B. Anyway.#This is too casual and unclear.
~C. I forgot the next part.#This weakens delivery because it does not guide the audience from one speech idea to the next.
=D. After organizing the points, the speaker should add support for each idea.#Correct. This shows a relationship between organization and support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which note card would best support speaking?
{
~A. Full paragraph copied from source.#This may make the speaker read instead of present.
~B. Stuff about presentations.#This is too vague because the note does not tell the speaker what point or support to deliver.
~C. Random words with no order.#This does not guide delivery because the speaker needs ordered cues that match the speech plan.
=D. Point 2: paraphrase support; reasoning: audience hears source idea clearly.#Correct. This gives a clear speaking cue.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan has coherent focus?
{
=A. Focus: speech preparation; points: outline, paraphrase support, rehearse.#Correct. Each point supports preparation.
~B. Focus: speech preparation; points: lunch, phones, weather.#These points do not support the focus.
~C. Focus: note cards; points: cafeteria, sports, music.#These points are unrelated.
~D. Focus: presentation clarity; points: shoes, snacks, weekend plans.#These points do not support clarity.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which sentence is reasoning?
{
~A. A note card can list each main point.#This is support, not reasoning, because it gives a detail without explaining why it matters.
=B. This helps because the speaker can find the next idea without reading a full script.#Correct. This explains why the support matters.
~C. The next point is rehearsal.#This is a transition because it moves to another idea instead of explaining why support matters.
~D. Notes are paper.#This does not explain support because it only names the material instead of explaining its value.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Clear transitions help listeners follow the relationship between ideas. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Transitions confuse listeners.#This reverses the source meaning.
=B. Transitions guide the audience by showing how speech ideas connect.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning in new wording.
~C. Listeners do not need transitions.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Relationships between ideas should be hidden.#This is the opposite of the source.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What makes a speech plan presentation-ready?
{
~A. It contains only a topic.#A topic alone is incomplete.
~B. It uses random facts.#Random facts weaken the plan.
=C. It has required parts arranged clearly for speaking.#Correct. Speech-ready format supports clear delivery.
~D. It hides the conclusion.#The conclusion should be clear.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which conclusion best closes an informative speech about preparation?
{
~A. I also like weekends.#This adds an unrelated idea.
=B. Organized preparation helps speakers plan, support, and deliver clearer presentations.#Correct. This restates the focus and summarizes key ideas.
~C. There are many topics.#This is too vague because it does not restate the speech focus or help the audience remember the message.
~D. I am done now.#This does not reinforce the message.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support should be removed from a speech about presentation preparation?
{
~A. Rehearsing aloud helps speakers notice unclear sections.#This supports preparation.
~B. A note card can list main points in order.#This supports preparation.
~C. A transition can connect support to the next point.#This supports speech planning.
=D. Cafeteria lines are sometimes long.#Correct. This is unrelated to presentation preparation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which paraphrase is too close to the source "Speakers should practice aloud before presenting"?
{
=A. Speakers should practice aloud before presenting.#Correct. This copies the source exactly, so it is too close to count as an effective paraphrase.
~B. Presenters should rehearse out loud before giving the speech.#This changes wording while keeping meaning, so it is an acceptable paraphrase.
~C. Practicing out loud helps a speaker prepare to present.#This restates the idea with new wording instead of copying the source exactly.
~D. Rehearsal before presenting can support delivery.#This changes wording and structure.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which format problem should be fixed first?
{
~A. The plan has a clear focus.#This is a strength, not a problem.
~B. The plan has support under each point.#This is a strength because support helps each main point become clear and complete.
=C. The plan has no main points.#Correct. Main points are required for a speech plan.
~D. The plan has transitions.#Transitions support quality.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which structure best fits explaining how to prepare a speech?
{
~A. Random order.#Random order would confuse the audience.
~B. Compare unrelated topics.#This does not explain a process.
=C. Chronological order.#Correct. Speech preparation is a step-by-step process.
~D. Hidden order.#A hidden order does not guide listeners.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which answer best combines support and reasoning?
{
~A. Rehearsal is helpful.#This is too general because it does not include a specific support detail or reasoning.
=B. A timer can measure a speech section, which helps the speaker revise parts that run too long.#Correct. This gives a detail and explains why it matters.
~C. Speeches can be timed.#This is a detail but does not explain the value.
~D. Timing is important because timing is important.#This repeats instead of reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which choice is best for speech notes?
{
~A. Full script copied word for word.#This may lead to reading.
~B. No notes at all.#No notes may weaken organization.
=C. Short cues for focus, points, support, and transitions.#Correct. Short cues support delivery.
~D. Unrelated phrases.#Unrelated phrases do not guide speaking.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Why is paraphrasing useful in an informative speech plan?
{
~A. It lets the speaker change the source meaning.#Changing meaning is inaccurate.
=B. It helps the speaker explain source ideas in their own words.#Correct. Paraphrase supports clear, accurate speech notes.
~C. It removes all support from the speech.#Paraphrase can be a form of support.
~D. It requires copying the source exactly.#Copying is not paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which question checks coherent focus?
{
=A. Does each main point support the same central idea?#Correct. This directly checks coherence.
~B. Is every sentence copied from a source?#Copying does not check focus.
~C. Is the speech as long as possible?#Length does not prove focus.
~D. Are the ideas random enough?#Random ideas weaken focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition signals sequence?
{
~A. However, another support detail is needed.#However signals contrast, so it does not show the order of steps in a sequence.
~B. Because support explains the point.#Because signals cause, so it does not signal sequence.
=C. First, organize the main points before adding support.#Correct. First signals sequence.
~D. In contrast, source notes are different.#In contrast signals comparison.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which checklist item supports quality work?
{
~A. The plan hides its focus.#A hidden focus weakens quality.
~B. The notes are too vague to use.#Vague notes weaken delivery.
=C. The conclusion restates the informative focus.#Correct. A conclusion should close the speech clearly.
~D. The support is unrelated.#Unrelated support weakens quality.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which option best explains why support matters?
{
~A. Support makes the speech longer only.#Length is not the main purpose.
~B. Support replaces the focus.#The focus is still needed.
~C. Support lets the speaker avoid reasoning.#Reasoning is still needed.
=D. Support gives the audience details that explain the point.#Correct. Support helps listeners understand the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_QZ_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which statement best summarizes Lesson 7?
{
=A. An informative speech plan should combine focus, organization, support, paraphrase, transitions, and format.#Correct. This captures the integrated planning goal.
~B. A speech plan only needs a topic.#A topic alone is incomplete.
~C. Support and transitions are optional for clear explanation.#They are important for audience understanding.
~D. Paraphrase means changing the source meaning.#Paraphrase must keep meaning accurate.
}
